{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"amazon-ec2-image-builder","skill_description":"Creates and automates custom image builds with EC2 Image Builder - Linux, Windows, and macOS AMIs, and container images to ECR. Covers the build IAM role, Amazon-managed and custom components, image recipes, infrastructure and distribution configuration (launch templates, SSM parameters, other Regions), one-off builds, recurring scheduled pipelines for golden AMI automation and OS patching, custom image workflows, and diagnosing failed builds. Applies when creating, automating, or scheduling AMI or container image builds with Image Builder, or when debugging a failed build. Not for launching instances from existing AMIs, AMI lifecycle/retirement, or general EC2 fleet management.\n\nServices: imagebuilder, ec2, ecr, iam\nTasks: create, configure, deploy, debug\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: compute, containers\n*","skill_name":"amazon-ec2-image-builder"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"AWS Glue Data Quality now supports rule labeling for enhanced reporting - AWS","context":"AWS Glue Data Quality has introduced a new feature called rule labeling, which allows users to apply custom labels to data quality rules for better organization, filtering, and reporting. This feature enables categorizing rules based on business context, team ownership, compliance requirements, or any custom taxonomy. Rule labels can be queried to identify failing rules within specific categories, count rule outcomes by team or domain, and generate focused reports for different stakeholders. Users can label rules as part of the Data Quality Definition Language (DQDL) and query labels as part of rule outcomes, row-level results, and API responses. Rule labeling is available in all commercial AWS Regions where AWS Glue Data Quality is available. To learn more about rule labeling, refer to the AWS Glue Data Quality documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/11/glue-data-quality-rule-labeling-enhanced-reporting/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"AWS Deadline Cloud now supports configurable job scheduling modes for queues - AWS","context":"AWS Deadline Cloud, a managed service for simplifying render management for computer-generated graphics and visual effects, has introduced configurable job scheduling modes for queues. Previously, all available workers were assigned to the highest-priority, earliest-submitted job, potentially delaying feedback on other jobs. Now, users can select from three scheduling modes when creating or updating a queue: priority FIFO (the default), priority balanced, and weighted balanced. Priority balanced distributes workers evenly across all jobs at the highest priority level, while weighted balanced assigns jobs based on configurable parameters including priority, error count, submission time, and rendering task count. These new scheduling modes enable artists to receive immediate feedback on their submissions without waiting for earlier jobs to complete. The feature is available in all AWS Regions where Deadline Cloud is supported. To get started, visit the Deadline Cloud developer guide","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/04/deadline-cloud-job-scheduling/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"AWS Config launches 75 new managed rules - AWS","context":"AWS Config has introduced 75 new managed rules to support various use cases such as security, durability, and operations. These rules can be searched, discovered, enabled, and managed directly from AWS Config. The new rules cover services like AWS Amplify, Amazon SageMaker, Amazon Route 53, and more. Users can assess their security posture across multiple AWS services and deploy controls across an account or organization using Conformance Packs. The following is a list of the newly launched rules:\n\n1. ACM_CERTIFICATE_TRANSPARENT_LOGGING_ENABLED\n2. AMPLIFY_APP_BUILD_SPEC_CONFIGURED\n3. AMPLIFY_APP_PLATFORM_CHECK\n4. AMPLIFY_BRANCH_AUTO_BUILD_ENABLED\n5. AMPLIFY_BRANCH_BUILD_SPEC_CONFIGURED\n6. AMPLIFY_BRANCH_FRAMEWORK_CONFIGURED\n7. AMPLIFY_BRANCH_PULL_REQUEST_PREVIEW_ENABLED\n8. APIGATEWAY_DOMAIN_NAME_TLS_CHECK\n9. APIGATEWAYV2_INTEGRATION_PRIVATE_HTTPS_ENABLED\n10. APPINTEGRATIONS_APPLICATION_APPROVED_ORIGINS_CHECK\n11. APPINTEGRATIONS_APPLICATION_TAGGED\n12. APPMESH_MESH_IP_PREF_CHECK\n13. APPMESH_VIRTUAL_GATEWAY_LISTENERS_HEALTH_CHECK_ENABLED\n14. APPMESH_VIRTUAL_NODE_LISTENERS_HEALTH_CHECK_ENABLED\n15. APPMESH_VIRTUAL_NODE_LISTENERS_OUTLIER_DETECT_ENABLED\n16. APPMESH_VIRTUAL_NODE_SERVICE_BACKENDS_TLS_ENFORCED\n17. CLOUDTRAIL_EVENT_DATA_STORE_MULTI_REGION\n18. CLOUDWATCH_ALARM_DESCRIPTION\n19. CODEARTIFACT_REPOSITORY_TAGGED\n20. CODEBUILD_PROJECT_TAGGED\n21. EC2_IPAMSCOPE_TAGGED\n22. EC2_LAUNCHTEMPLATE_EBS_ENCRYPTED\n23. ECS_SERVICE_PROPAGATE_TAGS_ENABLED\n24. ELBV2_TARGETGROUP_HEALTHCHECK_PROTOCOL_ENCRYPTED\n25. ELBV2_TARGETGROUP_PROTOCOL_ENCRYPTED\n26. EVENTSCHEMAS_DISCOVERER_TAGGED\n27. EVENTSCHEMAS_REGISTRY_TAGGED\n28. GROUNDSTATION_CONFIG_TAGGED\n29. GROUNDSTATION_DATAFLOWENDPOINTGROUP_TAGGED\n30. GROUNDSTATION_MISSIONPROFILE_TAGGED\n31. HEALTHLAKE_FHIRDATASTORE_TAGGED\n32. IAM_OIDC_PROVIDER_CLIENT_ID_LIST_CHECK\n33. IAM_POLICY_DESCRIPTION\n34. IMAGEBUILDER_DISTRIBUTIONCONFIGURATION_TAGGED\n35. IMAGEBUILDER_IMAGEPIPELINE_TAGGED\n36. IMAGEBUILDER_IMAGERECIPE_EBS_VOLUMES_ENCRYPTED\n37. IMAGEBUILDER_IMAGERECIPE_TAGGED\n38. IMAGEBUILDER_INFRASTRUCTURECONFIGURATION_TAGGED\n39. KINESISVIDEO_SIGNALINGCHANNEL_TAGGED\n40. KINESISVIDEO_STREAM_TAGGED\n41. LAMBDA_FUNCTION_APPLICATION_LOG_LEVEL_CHECK\n42. LAMBDA_FUNCTION_LOG_FORMAT_JSON\n43. LAMBDA_FUNCTION_SYSTEM_LOG_LEVEL_CHECK\n44. LIGHTSAIL_BUCKET_OBJECT_VERSIONING_ENABLED\n45. MEDIAPACKAGE_PACKAGINGCONFIGURATION_TAGGED\n46. MEDIATAILOR_PLAYBACKCONFIGURATION_TAGGED\n47. M","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/02/aws-config-launches-new-rules/"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"AWS Network Firewall adds wildcard support for container attribute filters - AWS","context":"AWS Network Firewall has introduced wildcard support for container attribute filters in Amazon EKS and Amazon ECS. This update enables users to define attribute filters using wildcard patterns to match multiple container workloads in a single rule. The wildcard pattern can automatically cover all variants of an application, simplifying firewall rule management in dynamic container environments where new application variants are frequently deployed. This enhancement is available in all AWS Regions where AWS Network Firewall container attribute-based inspection is supported. To utilize this feature, users can visit the AWS Network Firewall product page and service documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/10/aws-network-firewall-container-attributes-wildcard/"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify how ECR lifecycle policy rule priority evaluation excludes already-matched images", "parent_id": "b0c3930a-edd3-471a-b1b5-5a544b16804d"}
